# Environment variables — which file gets what
#
# Two files, one rule: never define the same key in both.
#
#   .env         Neon CLI territory. `neon link`, `neon checkout` and
#                `neon deploy` rewrite DATABASE_URL, DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED and
#                NEON_BRANCH here on every run. Do not hand-edit it, and do not
#                put anything else in it — it is not yours.
#
#   .env.local   Yours. Clerk, Vercel Blob, anything hand-written. No tool
#                touches it. Next.js loads it at HIGHER precedence than .env,
#                which is why a stray DATABASE_URL here silently overrides the
#                Neon-managed one and points the app at a different database
#                from the Prisma CLI.
#
# Precedence, highest first:
#   1. a variable exported in your shell  (beats every file — check this first
#                                          when a connection makes no sense)
#   2. .env.local
#   3. .env
#
# Both files are gitignored. This example file is not; keep real values out.


# A note on the placeholders below: they are written as <descriptions> rather
# than realistic-looking strings on purpose. GitHub push protection scans for
# key *shapes*, and "sk_test_" followed by filler characters trips its Stripe
# test-key detector — it blocked this very file once. Keep them descriptive.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .env  — written by the Neon CLI, shown here only so you know what to expect
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Pooled connection. This is the one the app uses.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-HOST-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/DBNAME?sslmode=require&channel_binding=require"

# Direct connection, no pooler. For migrations and anything needing a session.
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-HOST.REGION.aws.neon.tech/DBNAME?sslmode=require&channel_binding=require"

# The linked branch. Set by `neon link`.
NEON_BRANCH="production"

# Do NOT add connection_limit or pool_timeout — lib/prisma.ts appends them.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .env.local — copy this section into .env.local and fill it in
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Clerk — dashboard.clerk.com → API Keys
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY="<your Clerk publishable key — begins pk_test_>"
CLERK_SECRET_KEY="<your Clerk secret key — begins sk_test_>"

# Clerk webhook signing secret — Webhooks → your endpoint → Signing Secret.
# Optional locally: getOrCreateUser() creates the User row as a fallback.
CLERK_WEBHOOK_SECRET="<your Clerk webhook signing secret — begins whsec_>"

# All four point at "/" on purpose. The landing page reads the session and
# sends each role to its own dashboard; hardcoding /student-dashboard here
# would send admins somewhere they get bounced out of.
# Where the app is reachable; used for the admin-invitation link Clerk emails.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_URL="/sign-in"
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_UP_URL="/sign-up"
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_AFTER_SIGN_IN_URL="/"
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_AFTER_SIGN_UP_URL="/"
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_FALLBACK_REDIRECT_URL="/"
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_UP_FALLBACK_REDIRECT_URL="/"

# Vercel Blob — profile photos, company logos, JD PDFs, import files.
# Optional locally if you are not exercising uploads.
BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN="<your Vercel Blob token — begins vercel_blob_rw_>"

# Shown on the Access Revoked page, so a removed admin knows whom to write to.
# Optional: without it the page points them to the placement office.
# SUPPORT_CONTACT_EMAIL="placement@your-college.edu"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NODE_ENV — deliberately absent
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Do not set NODE_ENV in any env file. Next sets it itself: development for
# `next dev`, production for `next build` and `next start`. An inherited
# NODE_ENV=development makes `next build` fail while prerendering the built-in
# error pages, and the error points nowhere near the cause:
#
#   Error: <Html> should not be imported outside of pages/_document
#
# lib/env.ts defaults it when absent, so leaving it out is safe.
